course: medicine
lesson: 64
title: Cheating and doping, as reported
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Doping means using banned drugs or methods to get an edge, and it cheats twice: it robs the clean athletes who lost, and it risks the cheat's own body, because drugs strong enough to change a body can harm the heart, liver and growth.
explain: Jack wears his dad's old yellow Tour jersey, «Armstrong won seven Tours, legend», and his dad says «Read up on it, son»: as widely reported, Lance Armstrong was stripped of all seven titles in 2012 and admitted doping in 2013.
explain: Cheats can't relax: testers can turn up at home or at training without warning, samples are stored for up to ten years and tested again with newer methods, and some London 2012 medals were taken back years later and given to the athletes who had been cheated.
explain: Cheating isn't only drugs: a handball goal, sandpaper on a cricket ball or a hidden motor in a bike all break the rules, whether the referee sees it or not.
fact: As widely reported, Lance Armstrong was stripped of his seven Tour de France titles in 2012 for doping and admitted it in 2013.
fact: Drugs like EPO thicken the blood, which can raise the risk of dangerous clots.
fact: Investigations including the 2016 McLaren report described state-backed doping in Russia, including swapped urine samples at the Sochi 2014 Winter Olympics; Russian athletes later competed under neutral names.
fact: Ben Johnson won the 1988 Olympic 100 m in Seoul in 9.79 seconds but was stripped of the gold days later after testing positive for a steroid, and the gold went to Carl Lewis.
fact: Athletes who are tested are responsible for anything in their body, and some cold medicines and supplements contain banned substances, so they check every medicine with a doctor or pharmacist.
fact: Top athletes must tell testers where they will be for a 1-hour slot every day of the year, and testing also happens out of competition without warning.
fact: Doping samples can be stored for up to 10 years and retested; several London 2012 medals were later taken back and passed to other athletes.
fact: Steroids are not protein; they can damage the heart and liver, cause bad acne and mood swings, and in teenagers stop growth early.
fact: A serious first doping offence can bring a ban of up to 4 years.
fact: In the 1986 World Cup quarter-final Diego Maradona scored with his hand against England and the goal stood; Argentina won 2–1, and today VAR checks goals for handball.
fact: In 2018 in Cape Town, Australian cricketers were caught changing the ball with sandpaper; Steve Smith and David Warner were banned for 12 months and Cameron Bancroft for 9.
fact: In 2016 a cyclist was found with a hidden motor in her bike at the cyclo-cross world championships and was banned for 6 years.
fact: Anyone offered something to «bulk up» should say no and tell an adult.
exercise: Jack: «Armstrong won seven Tours, legend». What happened, as reported? | He was stripped of all seven for doping | He kept all seven titles | He won an eighth one later | He was never tested at all
exercise: In which year did Armstrong admit doping? | 2013 | 1999 | 2005 | 2020
exercise: Why can a blood-thickening drug like EPO be dangerous? | It can raise the risk of clots | It makes the blood too thin | It stops the heart beating at once | It is only harmful to bikes
exercise: Olivia: «Why did Russia's athletes have a different name at the Olympics?» | Investigations reported state-backed doping | They changed their country's name for the Games | They asked to be called something new | Russia was not invited to any sport
exercise: Where were urine samples reported swapped? | Sochi 2014 Winter Olympics | London 2012 Summer Olympics | Seoul 1988 | Tokyo 2020
exercise: Grandpa: «Seoul, 1988. Ben Johnson ran 9.79». What happened days later? | He was stripped after testing positive | He broke the world record again a week later | He won a second gold in the relay race too | He retired as champion and kept the medal
exercise: Who got the 1988 100 m gold instead? | Carl Lewis | Usain Bolt | Ben Johnson's coach | Nobody, it was left empty
exercise: Aoife's sister took Mam's cold tablets before a gala. What should tested athletes do? | Check every medicine with a doctor or pharmacist | Take any cold medicine they like | Take double to be safe | Hide medicines from the testers
exercise: Sam: «The drug testers only come on race day». What's true? | They can turn up anytime without warning | They only test at the Olympics | They only test winners on the day | They only test the athletes who come last in a race
exercise: How long must top athletes be findable each day? | A 1-hour slot every day | 24 hours a day, at all times | 1 hour a month, on a set day | Only on race days and nowhere else
exercise: Ava: «She got her medal years later?» How is that possible? | Stored samples were retested and cheats caught | The post was just very slow | She asked for a new one | Medals are handed out every 10 years
exercise: How long can doping samples be stored? | Up to 10 years | Up to 10 days | Up to 1 year | Only until the race ends
exercise: Kieran repeats «steroids are just protein, innit». What's true? | Steroids are strong drugs with serious side effects | Steroids are a kind of protein shake | Steroids are a healthy vitamin | Steroids are only dangerous for adults
exercise: Uncle: «Hand of God my backside, bloody handball!» What did Maradona do in 1986? | Scored with his hand against England | Saved a penalty with his head | Scored from his own half | Got sent off for a handball in the first half
exercise: Lachie: «Bit of sandpaper on the ball, so what, mate?» What happened in 2018? | Players were banned for up to 12 months | Nothing, roughing up the ball is allowed | The team won a prize for clever thinking | The ball was renamed and kept in a museum
exercise: Lily: «A motor in a bike? Proper genius». Dad: «Proper banned». What happened in 2016? | The cyclist got a 6-year ban | The cyclist won a medal | The motor was made legal | The bike was given to a museum
test: Jack: «Everyone was doing it back then, so it was fair». What's wrong with that? | It still cheated clean riders and risked health | If everyone does it, it's fair | Old rules don't count any more | Only winners need to follow rules
test: Kieran repeats it at school. A teacher hears. Best response for Kieran next time? | Never take anything to bulk up, tell an adult if offered | Try a little steroid to test it | Ask the gym for the strongest pills | Swap protein for steroids at weekends
test: Ben Johnson's 9.79 against Bolt's 9.58: what does a time you cheated for count? | Nothing, it was taken away | A world record that lasts forever | Half a record, because he did run it | It counts if nobody saw him take drugs
test: What would happen if a tested swimmer took a cold tablet with a banned substance by accident? | She could still break the rules and be punished | Nothing, accidents never count against a swimmer | She'd get a medal anyway | The tablet would cure the doping
test: Sam's mate: «Hide on race day and you're safe». Why won't that work? | Testers come out of competition too, and samples are kept | Testers only check shoes | Hiding is allowed in the rules | Nobody tests swimmers
test: A London 2012 cheat thinks: «Games over, I got away with it». What could happen up to 2022? | A retest could catch them | Nothing, results are frozen | They get a bonus medal | Samples are always thrown away
test: Tomás, 10: «Can you both shut it?» His dad says «the ref didn't see it, so it's fair». True? | Breaking the rules is cheating, seen or not | It's fair if the ref misses it | Handballs are allowed in finals | Only fouls are cheating, handballs are just bad luck
test: Lachie calls sandpaper «harmless». Which shows it wasn't? | Bans of 12, 12 and 9 months | A small fine for damaging the ball | A warning letter only | Nothing happened at all
test: Olivia's friend: «It was just a few bad athletes». What did the investigations describe? | An organised system | One athlete acting alone | A mistake by the testers | Nothing, it was all a rumour
test: A cartoon cyclist says «Using tech isn't doping, so it's not cheating». Correct it. | A hidden motor is cheating, like drugs | Tech is always allowed | Motors help everyone fairly, so they're not cheating | Only drugs count as cheating
test: Bans for a serious first doping offence can be up to 4 years. How many months is that? | 48 | 12 | 24 | 40
test: Aoife's Mam: «Ah, Jesus, show me the box». Why does Mam want the box? | To check what's in the medicine | To take a photo of the box for fun | To throw the box away in the bin | To buy more of it at the chemist
exam: Identify the consequence which befell Lance Armstrong in 2012, as widely reported. | He was stripped of his seven Tour de France titles | He was awarded an eighth Tour de France title in retrospect | He was appointed head of drug testing | He was exonerated of all allegations
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle governing substances found in a tested athlete? | The athlete is responsible for any substance in the body | The athlete is responsible only for deliberate and knowing use | The coach alone bears responsibility | No responsibility attaches to the athlete
exam: It is given that a doping sample was collected in 2012 and may be retained for ten years. Determine the final year in which it may be retested. | 2022 | 2014 | 2032 | 2012
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning anabolic steroids. | They may damage the heart and liver and halt growth in adolescents | They are a form of dietary protein | They are entirely free of side effects | They are recommended for growing children
